  "subject_sha": "47deacafca914a2051294b0a5999eb6268b31e86",
  "verdict": "changes-requested",
  "summary": "Read-only review of 47deacaf (round 2/4). The two fixture fixes handle the root causes described in the validation report. The ACL fixture now changes only the DACL through [IO.File]::GetAccessControl/SetAccessControl(Access), without Get-Acl/Set-Acl or SACL privileges, and asserts that owner and AreAccessRulesProtected are unchanged. The bootstrap fixture uses -ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned only for its synthetic child (no Bypass or Set-ExecutionPolicy). It keeps all seven scenarios with their HEAD, file, marker and reason assertions, and compares the MachinePolicy/UserPolicy/LocalMachine/CurrentUser execution policies before and after. No assertions were removed and no skips were added; the existing skips only apply off Windows. Production installer/runner/bootstrap code is unchanged, so the earlier security properties still hold: private ACL creation, inspection before mutation, no task overwrite, provider tokens stripped, no shell. Evidence: the report records native 10/10 runs (root and independent) with source manifests, a runtime snapshot (PT5M, Interactive/Limited, Ready, last result 0) and the original two failures with their causes. The controller-supplied CI context shows windows-review-setup and bug-pipeline-tests passing at this SHA; the gate check is still in progress. The 13 broad-suite failures on the Windows host are disclosed, not waived, and are outside this fixture-only change. The tracked packet does not include the raw native logs; they are referenced by path. I did not run any tests. One small test-strength gap remains on the 'proves public ACL rejected' criterion.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "low",
      "file": "agent-team/tests/bug-pipeline/windows-review.test.mjs",
      "line": 296,
      "problem": "The negative privacy check only asserts `.ok === false`. It never checks why the privacy script failed. Any PowerShell error would satisfy it: module or host failure, Get-Item failure, the owner check, or an encoding error. Acceptance criterion 1 requires proof that the public (Everyone Read) ACL specifically is rejected, so a regression that broke the DACL-rule check could hide behind an unrelated failure.",
      "requested_change": "Capture the result of the second privacy run. Assert ok === false and that its stderr (or stdout) matches /Non-private scheduler state/, the throw for a foreign Allow rule in the privacy taskScript (scripts/automation/install-claude-review-windows.mjs, privacy branch). Optionally, before running privacy, confirm the mutation actually added an S-1-1-0 rule by reading the DACL back in the same `change` program.",
      "test_expectation": "Native Windows run: 'native Windows ACL creation is private and rejects a later public access rule' passes only when privacy rejects with the 'Non-private scheduler state' reason. Temporarily removing the IdentityReference/sid comparison from the privacy script would make the test fail. Native suite still 10/10 with no skips on Windows, and the windows-review-setup CI job is green on the new SHA."
    }
  ]
}

